abstract: >
  A reproducible, commit-traced benchmark dataset of actual blockchain software
  development effort, built to calibrate and test COCOMO-style software cost
  estimation models for blockchain projects. 44 independently verified
  calibration points (73 raw source rows before dependent-window collapse of
  repeated measurements of the same project). Candidates were screened from
  four contributing sources (the Web3 Foundation Grants Program, the aeternity
  community forum, Polkadot OpenGov and treasury governance records, and Zcash
  Community Grants) plus two further sources that were fully screened and
  correctly produced zero eligible rows (Crust Grants and the Polkadot Open
  Source Developer Grants program, both because their delivered projects report
  only planned effort, not the actual effort this dataset requires). Each point
  pairs a reported actual development-effort figure with an independently
  measured code size (KSLOC, reuse-adjusted) of the exact delivered repository
  at the exact delivered commit. Constructed via a PRISMA-style identification,
  screening, eligibility, and inclusion pipeline, with the same admission rule
  applied to every candidate regardless of source, and raw sources frozen with
  SHA-256 checksums for independent verification.
